## Housekeeping

### 1. Why `@/` failed (and the proper fix, optional)

Vitest doesn't automatically read the `paths` section of `tsconfig.json` — it's a Vite-based runner with its own resolver. Your relative-import workaround is perfectly fine. If you'd rather keep `@/` consistency in future test files, create `vitest.config.ts` in the project root:

```ts
import { defineConfig } from "vitest/config";
import path from "node:path";

export default defineConfig({
  resolve: {
    alias: { "@": path.resolve(import.meta.dirname, ".") },
  },
});
```

(If your Node is older than 20.11 and `import.meta.dirname` errors, use `path.resolve("./")` instead.)

### 2. A mistake of mine that *your* output exposed — I'm correcting myself

Look closely at your test names: `(limit long @ 500 with spot 1)` … `with spot 0`. **There is no spot 1 or spot 0 — spot is always 590.** My `it.each` title had four placeholders (`%s %s @ %d with spot %d`) but each data row only carried four values where the last was the expected-validity *boolean*. Vitest formatted `true`/`false` as `1`/`0` into the "spot" slot. The assertions themselves ran correctly against 590 (that's why everything passed) — but the labels lie. You asked me never to assume and always to verify; this is me holding myself to the same standard. Cosmetic-only fix if you want pretty output:

```ts
it.each([
  ["limit",  "long",  500, true ],
  ["limit",  "long",  590, true ],
  ["limit",  "long",  600, false],
  ["limit",  "short", 600, true ],
  ["limit",  "short", 580, false],
  ["stop",   "long",  600, true ],
